            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'zh'">
                <p>新品上线 TikTok Shop，最怕的不是产品没人看，而是素材测完一轮还没起量。冷启动阶段的核心任务就一件事：用最短的时间找到能跑起来的素材组合。素材池怎么搭、搭多大，这里直接给答案。</p>
<h2>素材池到底要准备多少条？</h2>
<p><strong>判断标准：冷启动期至少准备15-30条原创视频素材。</strong></p>
<p>低于15条，基本等于把命运押在1-2个素材上，容错率太低。超过30条，如果团队创意和拍摄资源跟不上，质量会明显下滑，反而浪费预算。</p>
<p>实际操作中，建议分两批准备：第一批10-15条快速测试，用已有脚本和低成本拍摄；第二批根据跑出来的数据追加5-15条，放大有效方向。</p>
<hr />
<h2>不同阶段的素材类型怎么分配？</h2>
<p><strong>答案：测试期以UGC口播和情景剧为主，验证跑通后逐步加入产品特写和品牌叙事类。</strong></p>
<p>冷启动第一周，TikTok算法更偏好真实感和互动性。纯产品展示类的视频点击率和完播率往往不如真人出镜的口播或情景短剧。具体比例参考：</p>
<ul>
<li>UGC风格素材：60%</li>
<li>产品功能展示：25%</li>
<li>品牌故事/工厂场景：15%</li>
</ul>
<p>这个比例不是死的，跑出数据后根据CTR和CVR动态调整。如果你的品类天然适合视觉展示（如美妆、家居），产品特写比例可以适当提高。</p>
<hr />
<h2>UGC素材和品牌自产素材怎么平衡？</h2>
<p><strong>判断：UGC素材承担引流和测款，品牌素材负责信任背书和转化。两者都要有，但职能不同。</strong></p>
<p>很多团队踩的坑是：要么全部自己拍，画面精美但缺乏真实感，点击率上不去；要么全部丢给达人或素人，品质参差不齐，品牌调性混乱。</p>
<p>正确的做法是：</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>自产核心素材库</strong>：准备3-5条高质量产品视频，覆盖核心卖点，作为素材基底。</li>
<li><strong>UGC放大测试</strong>：把这3-5条基底素材拆解重组，配合不同脚本、不同出镜人物，快速生成10-20条测试素材。</li>
<li><strong>数据验证后固化</strong>：跑出高转化素材后，固定脚本和拍摄风格，形成可复用的模板。</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>测试期预算怎么分配？</h2>
<p><strong>原则：小预算多素材，广撒网，快速淘汰。</strong></p>
<p>单条素材日预算建议控制在20-50美元，跑3-5天看数据。重点关注两个指标：CTR（点击率）和CVR（转化率）。</p>
<ul>
<li>CTR低于1%的素材，优先淘汰。</li>
<li>CTR不错但CVR低于0.5%的，检查落地页或选品问题，不一定是素材的锅。</li>
</ul>
<p>总测试预算建议预留总冷启动预算的30%-40%。某工具类App团队的经验是：花2周时间、测50条素材，找到3-5条高效素材后集中放量，单用户获取成本下降了近40%。</p>
<hr />
<h2>怎么判断一条素材该不该继续投？</h2>
<p><strong>给一个可操作的判断框架：</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>数据维度</th>
<th>达标标准</th>
<th>处理方式</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CTR（点击率）</td>
<td>&gt;1.5%</td>
<td>继续观察</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>完播率</td>
<td>&gt;25%</td>
<td>内容有效</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CVR（转化率）</td>
<td>&gt;0.8%</td>
<td>素材有效，可放量</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>单订单成本</td>
<td>&lt;目标CAC的80%</td>
<td>保留，优化空间</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>如果一条素材跑满500美元预算，CTR仍低于0.8%，建议直接停掉，换方向。不要恋战。</p>
<hr />
<h2>素材本地化最容易忽略什么？</h2>
<p><strong>两个高频踩坑点：口音/语言习惯和消费场景适配。</strong></p>
<p>东南亚市场，英语不是万能的。同样说英语，印度用户和印尼用户的表达习惯差异很大。美国市场，南北方对同一款产品的使用场景理解也不同。</p>
<p>具体建议：</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>字幕优先</strong>：即使配音不完美，准确的本地化字幕能解决70%的理解问题。</li>
<li><strong>避免直译</strong>：产品卖点的表达要符合当地用户的思维方式。比如国内强调"大容量"，东南亚用户更在意"够用几天"。</li>
<li><strong>场景本土化</strong>：同一个保温杯，美国素材放办公室场景，东南亚放摩托车骑行场景，转化效果可能差2-3倍。</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>总结：冷启动素材池搭建的最低配置</h2>
<p>回到最初的问题，新品冷启动素材池的最小可行配置是什么？</p>
<p><strong>15条视频 + 3种内容类型 + 2周测试周期 + 30%测试预算占比。</strong></p>
<p>这四件事做到位，基本能判断出产品在这个市场的潜力。如果测完一轮数据仍然很差，先别急着追加素材，检查一下选品和定价是否对路——有时候不是素材的问题，是产品和市场的匹配度问题。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>When launching a new product on TikTok Shop, the scariest scenario isn't low views—it's burning through your test budget without finding a winning creative. During cold launch, your only real job is this: identify which creative combinations work in the shortest time possible. Here's how to build your creative pool right.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How Many Creatives Do You Actually Need?</h2>
<p><strong>Rule of thumb: prepare at least 15-30 original video creatives for cold launch.</strong></p>
<p>Below 15, you're betting everything on 1-2 creatives—far too risky. Above 30, and if your team's production capacity can't keep up, quality drops, wasting your budget on mediocrity.</p>
<p>Practical approach: prepare in two batches. First batch: 10-15 creatives using existing scripts and low-cost production. Second batch: add 5-15 more based on what the data tells you, doubling down on what's working.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How Should Content Types Be Distributed Across Phases?</h2>
<p><strong>Answer: Lead with UGC talking-heads and scenario sketches during testing; add product close-ups and brand storytelling after you've validated winners.</strong></p>
<p>In TikTok's first week, the algorithm rewards authenticity and engagement. Pure product demos often underperform compared to real-person talking-heads or short situational clips. Rough ratio:</p>
<ul>
<li>UGC-style content: 60%</li>
<li>Product feature demos: 25%</li>
<li>Brand story/factory scenes: 15%</li>
</ul>
<p>This isn't set in stone—adjust based on CTR and CVR data. If your category naturally suits visual demonstration (beauty, home goods), increase the product showcase ratio.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How Do You Balance UGC vs. Brand-Made Creatives?</h2>
<p><strong>Judgment: UGC handles traffic and testing; brand creatives build trust and drive conversions. Both are necessary, but they serve different purposes.</strong></p>
<p>Common mistake: either producing overly polished brand content that lacks authenticity (low CTR), or relying entirely on达人/influencers with inconsistent quality and scattered brand voice.</p>
<p>The right approach:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Build a core brand asset library</strong>: Create 3-5 high-quality product videos covering core selling points—these are your foundations.</li>
<li><strong>Amplify with UGC testing</strong>: Repurpose these 3-5 foundations with different scripts, different presenters, rapidly generating 10-20 test creatives.</li>
<li><strong>Standardize after validation</strong>: Once high-converting creatives emerge, lock in the script and filming style as reusable templates.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>How Should You Allocate Test Budget?</h2>
<p><strong>Principle: small budget per creative, wide net, fast elimination.</strong></p>
<p>Set daily budget per creative at $20-50, run for 3-5 days, then read the data. Focus on two metrics: CTR (click-through rate) and CVR (conversion rate).</p>
<ul>
<li>Creatives with CTR below 1%: prioritize for elimination.</li>
<li>Creatives with decent CTR but CVR below 0.5%: investigate landing page or product-market fit issues—not necessarily the creative's fault.</li>
</ul>
<p>Reserve 30-40% of total cold launch budget for testing. One utility app team's experience: after 2 weeks testing 50 creatives and finding 3-5 high-performers, they concentrated spend on winners, cutting user acquisition cost by nearly 40%.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How Do You Decide Whether a Creative Should Keep Running?</h2>
<p><strong>A practical decision framework:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Benchmark</th>
<th>Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CTR (click-through rate)</td>
<td>&gt;1.5%</td>
<td>Continue observing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Video completion rate</td>
<td>&gt;25%</td>
<td>Content resonates</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CVR (conversion rate)</td>
<td>&gt;0.8%</td>
<td>Creative works, ready to scale</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cost per order</td>
<td>&lt;80% of target CAC</td>
<td>Keep, optimize further</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If a creative runs $500 with CTR still below 0.8%, pull it and pivot. Don't get attached.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Gets Overlooked in Creative Localization?</h2>
<p><strong>Two high-frequency pitfalls: accent/language nuance and consumer scenario mismatch.</strong></p>
<p>In Southeast Asia, English isn't one-size-fits-all. The same English phrase lands differently with Indian users versus Indonesian users. In the US, a product's use case varies dramatically between Southern and Northern markets.</p>
<p>Practical tips:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Prioritize subtitles</strong>: Even with imperfect voiceovers, accurate localized subtitles solve 70% of comprehension issues.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid literal translation</strong>: Express product selling points in ways that match local thinking. In China, "large capacity" sells; in Southeast Asia, users care more about "how many days will it last."</li>
<li><strong>Scenario localization</strong>: Same thermos, US creatives show office settings, Southeast Asian creatives show motorcycle commutes—conversion rates can differ by 2-3x.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Bottom Line: Minimum Viable Cold Launch Creative Pool</h2>
<p>Back to the core question—what's the minimum viable creative pool for cold launch?</p>
<p><strong>15 videos + 3 content types + 2-week test cycle + 30% test budget allocation.</strong></p>
<p>Get these four things right and you'll have enough signal to判断 product-market fit in a new market. If data remains poor after a full test round, don't rush to add more creatives. Check your product selection and pricing first—sometimes the problem isn't creative, it's product-market mismatch.</p>
